None legible. No registration number was quoted in any minute; night glare, distance and motion blur prevented all plate readings. There are therefore no automatic single reads to list.

## 5. NOTABLE EVENTS
- **Minute 86:** a construction road roller travelled on the public carriageway among live traffic.
- **Minutes 244 and 279:** camera feed failure — null records; those minutes could not be assessed.
- **Minute 314:** a white car with red/blue warning lights apparently escorting/towing a white sedan.
- **Minute 327:** a white sedan with a blue beacon/lightbar, official/police-type.
- **Minute 205:** ambulance-type van with flashing red/blue beacons passing centre-left; minute 127 a white ambulance-type van with flashing lights on the right service road; minute 140 a red/blue fire-truck-type service vehicle with lights on; minute 249 a white vehicle with blue emergency-style roof lights.
- **Minutes 518 and 528:** a train visible crossing the elevated viaduct; one or two figures on the elevated track at minute 523.
- **Minutes 543–559 (continuing 560–584):** a large turquoise/white "KHODAL RAJ" luxury coach parked at the left kerb in the live carriageway for roughly 40 minutes, with a group of 3–8 people standing by its door — an unusually long stationary presence.
- **Minutes 141–144:** a red/yellow tourist-style coach parked at the right "CHITRA" bus stop for the whole period.
- **Minutes 383–394 and 395–401:** a white bus/coach stationary on the left carriageway for an extended period, with a person moving around it (minute 387).
- **Minutes 494–497:** "ROPIA/RUPIA"-branded buses parked at the left kerb, one pulling out at minute 497.
- **Minutes 164 and 572:** tractors with trailers crossing/using the right service road.
- **Minutes 154–155:** water-sprinkler tanker parked at the kerb spraying water, then operating on the carriageway.
- **Minute 305:** a pedestrian in a reddish top walked onto the carriageway and crossed leftward in front of traffic — the most hazardous pedestrian movement recorded.

## 6. CROWD AND CONGESTION
No traffic congestion, queues or jams at any point — flow was free-flowing in every minute, light-to-moderate overnight and steady-to-heavy in the evening and morning. Pedestrian gatherings occurred at the left auto stand and stalls: 8–12 people at minutes 696–697, 15–20 at minute 702, and groups of roughly 2–15 recurring throughout; 3–8 people stood beside the parked "KHODAL RAJ" coach (minutes 543–559). None constituted a crowd incident.

## 7. TRAFFIC VIOLATIONS
- **Helmetless riders:** repeatedly suspected (resolution-limited), e.g. minutes 0–24 (many), 527, 557, 653; helmet status could not be confirmed at distance in most other minutes.
- **Pedestrians crossing the live carriageway on foot:** minutes 4, 10, 84 (two pedestrians walking in the carriageway/median), 109, 127, 133, 235, 269, 272, 273, 305 (in front of traffic), 352 (near median gap), 372, 415/424 (crossing the main carriageway), 506, 507, 522, 529, 532, 533, 535, 539, 549, 586, 596, 601, 619, 651.
- **Stationary vehicles in/near the live carriageway:** "KHODAL RAJ" coach (minutes 543–584), red/yellow coach at right kerb (minutes 141–144), white bus on left carriageway (minutes 383–401), buses holding the left turning lane/kerb (minutes 45, 25, 45).
- No wrong-way driving, red-light jumping or erratic driving was observed.

## 8. PATTERN OF ACTIVITY
The scene showed a normal 24-hour arterial-road cycle. Evening (21:00–midnight) was busiest: continuous heavy two-way mixed traffic with prominent bus movements, an active auto stand and steady pedestrian activity around the market, stalls and bus stop. From roughly midnight (minutes 180–510) traffic thinned to light, free-flowing overnight levels dominated by two-wheelers, white cars and sporadic goods vehicles and buses; the auto-rickshaw rank sat idle with few or no pedestrians, and several coaches/buses parked for long stretches at the kerbs. From about 05:30 (minute 510) activity rebuilt steadily — more autos, buses and pedestrians — and by 07:00–09:00 (minutes 585–717) it was steady peak-period mixed traffic with numerous city/BRTS/GSRTC buses, goods vehicles and 8–20 pedestrians at the auto stand. Recurring elements throughout: the left auto-rickshaw rank, white cars as the dominant car colour, damp/reflective road after recent rain, ambulance-type vans, and heavy oncoming headlight glare overnight that prevented all make, model and plate identification. Nothing suggested organised or suspicious activity.

## 9. QUIET PERIODS
The quietest stretches were the overnight hours, approximately minutes 205–510 (00:24–05:29), where traffic was consistently light and free-flowing, pedestrians were scarce or absent (only minutes 235, 250, 253, 263, 269, 272, 273, 281, 284, 285, 291, 294, 295, 305, 318, 352, 357, 372, 396, 415, 424, 490, 491, 500, 504, 506, 507 recorded any), and nothing unusual occurred apart from the events in Section 5. Minutes 244 and 279 were null records (feed failure). Pre-dawn minutes 334–452 and 485–509 were similarly quiet, with only parked cars, autos and occasional buses and trucks moving through.
